This is the first-order Taylor approximation of Eq.~\eqref{eq:level-statistics-unfold}: $\bar N(E_{n+1})-\bar N(E_n)\approx \bar N'(\bar E)\,(E_{n+1}-E_n)$, and $\bar N'=1/\gap$ by Eq.~\eqref{eq:level-statistics-gap}.

How large is the neglected part? Write $\Delta=E_{n+1}-E_n$, so that $E_{n+1}=\bar E+\Delta/2$ and $E_n=\bar E-\Delta/2$. Taylor expansion about $\bar E$ gives
\begin{equation}
\bar N\left(\bar E\pm\tfrac{\Delta}{2}\right)=\bar N(\bar E)\pm\bar N'\frac{\Delta}{2}+\bar N''\frac{\Delta^2}{8}\pm\bar N'''\frac{\Delta^3}{48}+\cdots,
\end{equation}
with all derivatives evaluated at $\bar E$. Subtracting the lower sign from the upper sign, the even-order terms cancel:
\begin{equation}
\bar N(E_{n+1})-\bar N(E_n)=\bar N'\Delta+\bar N'''\frac{\Delta^3}{24}+\cdots .
\end{equation}
Because we expand about the midpoint, the curvature term $\bar N''$ drops out, and the relative error of the shortcut is approximately $\bar N'''\Delta^2/(24\,\bar N')$. For the zeros, $\Nsm'(t)=\log(t/2\pi)/(2\pi)$ (Eq.~\eqref{eq:level-statistics-density}), $\Nsm''(t)=1/(2\pi t)$ and $\Nsm'''(t)=-1/(2\pi t^2)$, so
\begin{equation}
\text{relative error}\approx-\frac{\Delta^2}{24\,\bar t^{\,2}\log(\bar t/2\pi)} .
\label{eq:level-statistics-shortcuterr}
\end{equation}
The shortcut is therefore accurate whenever the spacing $\Delta$ is small compared with the height $\bar t$, which is true for every zero and becomes better and better higher up.
